  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "A school bus accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $30\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $2$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours before decelerating with a constant deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ until it stops. Which graph represents the described motion?",
  "worked_solution": "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the school bus starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $30\\,{\\text{km/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $30.$ \n\nHowever, because the school bus is only accelerating for the first $2$ hours, we stop the line at $t=2.$ At this time, the school bus's speed has increased by $2\\cdot 30=60\\text{\u00a0km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(2,60).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2126d713b00d66abb0d06e5878fdadc543c9c8a3a0000eb2f56b1048a2824ca4.png)\n\nThe school bus then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours. So, the speed remains at $60\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $2$ hours, until $t=2+2=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/6512a9d80ca9de16f5384031590b7ae5e58a9a2871bd5ac7aace1803f0d82603.png)\n\nAfter $t=4$, the school bus has a deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $-20.$ The third line segment stops when the school bus's speed returns to $0.$ To figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(4,60)$ and has a slope of $-20,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-60 & =-20(t-4) \\\\ v-60 & =-20t+80 \\\\ v & =-20t+140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the school bus is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-20t+140 \\\\ 0 & =-20t+140 \\\\ 20t & =140 \\\\ t & =\\frac{140}{20} \\\\ t & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(7,0).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2305c2241cbd9b7f8da60f9aa377f9068a4bb42cf57f38a2ad6203ba7f325d42.png)",
